Create Spec: I want a zoomable image viewer for NAND flash dump files. NAND Flash dump files are usually 50 to 500 GB large files, which contain NAND pages. In a given dump file, all NAND pages have exactly the same length, usually between 500 and 20000 bytes long. The pages are then grouped in blocks, which each contain 64, 128, 256, 512 or 1024 pages per blocks. The whole dump file contains an arbitrary number of blocks. For the visualisation, the bits of each byte should be displayed in black (1) and white (0) horizontally, the LSB on the right side, the MSB on the left side. The bytes of the page should be displayed horizontally, the first byte on the left side, the last byte on the right side. The blocks should be displayed vertically, the first page on the top the last page on the bottom. It would be nice to have a few pixels space between the pages and the blocks. The blocks should be arranged in a grid from top to bottom and then from left to right, so that the whole picture approximately has an aspect ratio of 4:3. The image viewer should use an impage pyramid algorithm, so that live panning and zooming like in Google Maps is possible for the user.  Preprocessing the whole dump into the image pyramid would take too long due to he huge size of the dump, the user wants to be able to open a dump file with the viewer and immediately see data and be able to start zooming. To achieve this startup time we need the following optimizations: We need a task queueing system with 3 queues: High priority, normal priority, low priority. The high priority queue is only filled with the requests for the tiles that are currenty in the viewport of the user. If the user pans or zooms away, those requests can be withdrawn again from the high priority queue. The normal priority queue is for tiles that are outside of the viewport, both in X/Y and Z axis, so also higher resolution and lower resolution tiles are requested here, this is to make sure that tiles that user might navigate to next will be prepared already. The low priority queue contains all unfinished tiles. We likely want one worker per CPU core available, the workers always first look for high priority queue items, then for normal priority queue items, and only if both those queues are empty, they work on low priority items. All the image tiles are being cached to PNG files in a ".cache" directory. The image viewer always starts in the upper left corner on the highest resolution tiles, to make sure that the user can see data as soon as possible. When a worker has to work on a tile with highest resolution, it calculates which fragments are needed from the dump file to combine it into the tile, then it loads those parts from the dump file, delivers the tile image to the viewer and writes the tile image to a cache file in PNG format. When a worker gets a request for a tile that is higher up in the image pyramid, it tries to load the tiles from the layer below it. If the tiles are not cached yet, it should send requests for them in the high priority queue for the other workers to help, but keep one request itself and work on that one directly itself.
